                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = s(\langle 1 \rangle)
            %em particular
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %em the
            monoid object”. Rather, the picture should roughly be that we have all of the standard diagrams describing a commutative monoid object, except that the various objects in the diagrams are
            = succeed "." do
